{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfb9BbkWTPMeSBRt8SZ5P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One bug, and the test catches it.\n\n- `calc.py:2`: `add` returns `a - b` instead of `a + b`. `add(2, 3)` returns `-1`.\n- `test_calc.py:2`: the `assert add(2, 3) == 5` is correct and fails against the current code with `AssertionError`, so `ok` never prints. The test is fine. The coverage is thin: it checks one input pair, so it wouldn't catch something like `a * b` (which also gives 6, not 5, so it does fail here, but `add(2, 2)` would pass under `*`). Add a second case such as `add(0, 0)` or `add(-1, 1)` if you want it to pin down addition.\n\nThe fix is `return a + b`. I changed nothing, as you asked.\n\nSTATUS: DONE"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":279,"cache_read_input_tokens":27174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":279},"output_tokens":261,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dee151f-71b7-45d4-8282-e5893142aa3e","uuid":"5e8be701-f655-4278-9fde-d9cea07903aa","timestamp":"2026-10-01T07:42:58.380Z","request_id":"req_011Cfb9BbMCUK4zvGQRryfEm"}
